<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting Bakery Conveyors</h2>
<p>Specify bakery conveyors by answering these questions for each production stage:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What is the product?</strong> Bread, cookies, crackers, pastries, or snacks? Each has different fragility, temperature, and handling requirements.</li>
<li><strong>What is the throughput?</strong> Products per minute, lane count, and line speed determine conveyor width, speed, and motor sizing.</li>
<li><strong>What is the temperature range?</strong> Oven bands need high-temp metal belts. Cooling conveyors need airflow-permeable mesh. Packaging conveyors run at ambient.</li>
<li><strong>What floor space is available?</strong> Limited floor space drives spiral solutions over straight-line conveyors.</li>
<li><strong>What cleaning method is required?</strong> Allergen-critical lines need tool-free belt removal and COP capability. Non-allergen lines may use dry brush cleaning.</li>
<li><strong>What packaging equipment connects downstream?</strong> Wrapper infeed speed, bagger loading method, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packer</a> requirements determine conveyor output specifications.</li>
</ol>
<p>For budgeting guidance, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>. Bakery conveyors range from $$ for simple ambient belt sections to $$$$ for spiral cooling systems with environmental controls.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What types of conveyors are used in bakeries?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Bakeries use cooling spiral conveyors, oven band conveyors, proofing conveyors, depositor infeed belts, pan conveyors, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line conveyors</a>. Each production stage requires different belt materials, temperature ratings, and speed controls.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a cooling spiral conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A cooling spiral conveyor moves baked products through a helical path inside a tower, providing extended <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">cooling</a> time in a small floor footprint. A single spiral replaces 200+ feet of straight-line cooling conveyor in as little as 100 square feet. Spirals can be ambient, forced-air, or refrigerated.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What belt materials work for bakery conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Wire mesh belts (stainless for ovens, carbon steel for cooling), modular plastic (acetal or polypropylene for ambient handling), polyurethane (FDA-compliant direct food contact), and silicone (non-stick for sticky doughs). Selection depends on temperature, product type, and food contact requirements.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do bakery conveyors meet food safety standards?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Bakery conveyors meet food safety through BISSC certification, FDA-compliant contact materials, open-frame construction for cleaning access, and tool-free belt removal. BISSC standards address cleanability, material selection, and hygienic design specific to baking equipment. See <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyor standards</a> for more detail.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What is a proofing conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A proofing conveyor transports dough through a temperature- and humidity-controlled enclosure (80-110 F, 70-85% RH) where yeast fermentation occurs. Spiral proofers maximize floor space; straight-line proofers suit smaller operations. Dwell times range from 30 to 90 minutes depending on dough type.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-6">What belt is used on a proofing conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-6" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Proofing conveyor belts are usually modular plastic (polypropylene or acetal) or stainless steel wire mesh, chosen to resist warm, humid conditions and allow COP cleaning. When dough rides directly on the belt, the belt is a food-contact surface and must be FDA-compliant and release dough cleanly. Spiral proofers need a spiral-rated belt.</p>

</div>
